From 910a32e550d106daec56ffa18d546ebe5a9902b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:59:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] The legs walk under a swing: a hero or a humanoid foe
 attacking on the move steps instead of sliding

A swing went onto the upper body only after the unit had been measured moving for 0.22 s,
counted again from nothing for every clip, so a light swing's whole wind-up and blow played the
clip's planted stance while the hero glided at 3 yd/s, and an ability's clip (always "full")
slid for its whole length. Now:

- The legs' motion is the unit's (`LegsMotion` on the `AnimDriver`, from the movement flags the
  animation plays and the unit's speed), so a unit already walking takes a swing on the torso from
  its first frame and keeps it there to the end (stopping, the torso finishes the swing while the
  legs settle into stand). Models without a SpineLow split (beasts) and the kick (95) stay whole.
- The swing's turn is shared up the spine (`RigPose::ramped`, folded last in the evaluator: half
  at SpineLow, all of it at the chest, rotation only; with nothing ramped the pose is Bevy's bit
  for bit).
- The spine eases round to take the whole strafe gap while a swing is layered
  (`BodyTwist::swing_want`), so the swing faces the aim while the legs keep the strafe's turn.
- A hit-stop on a layered swing holds the swing alone; the legs walk on.
- `ActionDash`'s fifth argument `"legs"`: a lunge keeps the legs walking the way the keys go (the
  wire keeps the dash's), so a strafing or backing hero's legs don't flick forward and back, and
  at their own pace (the keys' speed, or the replaced walk's), so the lunge's burst (5.6 yd/s for
  the third blow) doesn't flick a walk into a run and back.
- `PlayUnitAnimation`'s body `"auto"`: an ability's clip that layers; the whole body on
  `animLayer 0`. `HasActionCombat()` is 29.
- The `animLayer` CVar (default 1; 0 is the twenty-eighth round's look exactly) and the
  `animlayer <share>|tuned` console command to tune the spine's share on film.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
